helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3143222				0	tg	\<emph\>Numeric\</emph\> variables are automatically assigned the value "0" as soon as they are declared.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3144770				0	tg	Once you have declared a variable as a certain type, you cannot declare the variable under the same name again as a different type				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145217				0	tg	\<emph\>Boolean\</emph\> variables contain either the TRUE or the FALSE value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145364				0	tg	MyNumber_5=20				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145596				0	tg	DIM text$(5,4)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145632				0	tg	Boolean variables store only one of two values: TRUE or FALSE. A number 0 evaluates to FALSE, every other value evaluates to TRUE.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119				0	tg	Correct				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146966				0	tg	Integer variables range from -32768 to 32767. If you assign a floating-point value to an integer variable, the decimal places are rounded to the next integer. Integer variables are rapidly calculated in procedures and are suitable for counter variables in loops. An integer variable only requires two bytes of memory. "%" is the type-declaration character.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147126				0	tg	[My Number]=12				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147296				0	tg	Dim Variable@				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147317				0	tg	MyNumber5=15				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147345				0	tg	Number,Mine=12				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147615				0	tg	Dim Variable As Boolean				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148599				0	tg	a="TestString"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148736				0	tg	$[officename] Basic knows one- or multi-dimensional arrays, defined by a specified variable type. Arrays are suitable for editing lists and tables in programs. Individual elements of an array can be addressed through a numeric index.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148797				0	tg	A variable name can consist of a maximum of 255 characters. The first character of a variable name \<emph\>must\</emph\> be a letter A-Z or a-z. Numbers can also be used in a variable name, but punctuation symbols and special characters are not permitted, with exception of the underscore character ("_"). In $[officename] Basic variable identifiers are not case-sensitive. Variable names may contain spaces but must be enclosed in square brackets if they do.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149036				0	tg	DIM a$, b As Integer				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149185				0	tg	DIM text$(5 to 25)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149256				0	tg	Not valid, punctuation marks are not allowed				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149443				0	tg	To force declaration of variables, use the following command:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149531				0	tg	DIM a As String				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149546				0	tg	Arrays \<emph\>must\</emph\> be declared with the \<emph\>Dim\</emph\> statement. There are several ways to define the index range of an array:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
