helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3147008				0	tg	Choose \<emph\>File - Open\</emph\>. Select a Microsoft Office file in the $[officename] file open dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3147574				0	tg	MS PowerPoint, *.ppt, *.pps				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3147620				0	tg	$[officename] Writer				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3148453				0	tg	In the \<emph\>Standard file format\</emph\> area, first select a document type, then select the file type for saving.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3149580				0	tg	$[officename] Calc				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3149807	18			0	tg	From now on, if you save a document, the \<emph\>File type \</emph\>will be set according to your choice. Of course, you still can select another file type in the file save dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3150011				0	tg	You may delete the VBA macros from the Microsoft Office file on loading or on saving.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3150486				0	tg	Choose \<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01130000.xhp\" name=\"File - AutoPilot - Document Converter\"\>\<emph\>File - Wizards - Document Converter\</emph\>\</link\> to start the wizard.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577				0	tg	If you use macros in one of the applications and want to use the same functionality in the other application, you must edit the macros. $[officename] can load the macros that are contained within Microsoft Office files and you can then view and edit the macro code in the $[officename] \<link href=\"text/shared/main0600.xhp\" name=\"Basic IDE\"\>Basic IDE\</link\> editor.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3152801				0	tg	$[officename] can open and save documents in the Microsoft Office file formats.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3153144				0	tg	Open a Microsoft Office document that contains VBA macro code. Change only the normal contents (text, cells, graphics), and do not edit the macros. Save the document as a Microsoft Office file type. Open the file in Microsoft Office, and the VBA macros will run as before.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3153379				0	tg	In the \<emph\>File type\</emph\> box, select a Microsoft Office file format.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3153543				0	tg	MS Word, *.doc				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3153626				0	tg	$[officename] Impress				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3154898				0	tg	MS Excel, *.xls				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3154921				0	tg	Microsoft Office and $[officename] cannot run the same macro code. Microsoft Office uses VBA (Visual Basic for Applications) code, and $[officename] uses Basic code based on the $[officename] API (Application Program Interface) environment. Although the programming language is the same, the objects and methods are different.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3155342				0	tg	...will open in $[officename] module				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3155366				0	tg	Choose \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01130100.xhp\" name=\"Tools - Options - Load/Save - VBA Properties\"\>\<emph\>Tools - Options - Load/Save - VBA Properties\</emph\>\</link\> to set the VBA macro handling of $[officename].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
