helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090910.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tg	Атрибутҳо				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147341				0	tg	\<bookmark_value\>brackets and grouping in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>grouping and brackets in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147341				0	tg	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091100.xhp\" name=\"Brackets and Grouping\"\>Brackets and Grouping\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145207				0	tg	\\[, \\]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145241				0	tg	left ] x right [				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145414				0	tg	\\ldline, \\rdline				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145590				0	tg	All types of brackets, including those that are visible, permit empty group definition. The enclosed expression may therefore be empty.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146320				0	tg	Operator brackets, also known as Bra-kets (angle brackets with a vertical line in between), are common in Physics notation: "langle a mline b rangle" or "langle a mline b mline c over d mline e rangle." The height and positioning of the vertical lines always corresponds exactly to the enclosing brackets.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146934				0	tg	"size *2 size -5 a" would be double the starting size minus 5				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146962				0	tg	When typing example formulas into the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window, note that spaces are often required for correct structure.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147073				0	tg	x^{a_{size 8 n}}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147086				0	tg	x^{a_{size +2 n}}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147129				0	tg	The following examples, however, do not look identical:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147169				0	tg	Using "left" and "right" makes the above expression valid in $[officename] Math: "left [2, 3 right )". However, the brackets do not have any fixed size because they adjust to the argument. Setting a single bracket is a bit cumbersome. Therefore, there you can display single brackets with a fixed size by placing a "\\" (backslash) in front of normal brackets. These brackets then act like any other symbol and no longer have the special functionality of brackets; that is they do not work as group builders and their orientation corresponds to that of other symbols. See "size *2 \\langle x \\rangle" and "size *2 langle x rangle".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147252				0	tg	\\lfloor, \\rfloor				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147332				0	tg	a_{size +2 n}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147403				0	tg	There are a total of eight (8) different types of brackets available. The "ceil" and "floor" brackets are often used for rounding up or down the argument to the next integer: "lceil -3.7 rceil = -3" or "lfloor -3.7 rfloor = -4".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147514				0	tg	In this way, intervals like the one above can be built in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> without any problems: \\[2", "3\\) or "\\]2", "3\\[ (Attention: These quotation marks are part of the entry.)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147526				0	tg	This differs slightly for competing or mutually influencing attributes. This is often the case with font attributes. For example, which color does the b have in "color yellow color red (a + color green b)", or which size does it have in "size *4 (a + size /2 b)"? Given a base size of 12, does it have the size 48, 6 or even 24 (which could be seen as a combination)? The following are basic resolution rules, which will be followed consistently in the future. In general, the rules apply to all group operations. This only has a visible effect on the font attributes, like "bold", "ital", "phantom", "size", "color" and "font":				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147587				0	tg	Examples (with a base size of 12 and 50% for indexes):				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148734				0	tg	Exactly identical proportions with "size 18 a_n" and "size *1.5 a_n".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149030				0	tg	The combination of mismatched brackets, single brackets and repositioned left and right brackets occurs often in mathematical formulas. The following is a formula that will create an error when typed:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149054				0	tg	Braces "{}" are used to group expressions together to form one new expression. For example, "sqrt {x * y}" is the square root of the entire product x*y, while "sqrt x * y" is the square root of x multiplied by y. Braces do not require an extra space.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149297				0	tg	"font sans ( a + font serif b)"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149715				0	tg	\\{ or \\lbrace, \\} or \\rbrace				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149877				0	tg	left [ x right )				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150014				0	tg	left lbrace x right none				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150263				0	tg	\\lceil, \\rceil				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150342				0	tg	Note: the quotation marks in the examples are used to emphasize text and do not belong to the content of the formulas and commands.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150756				0	tg	\\(, \\)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150935				0	tg	The same rules apply to "left" and "right" as to the other brackets: they also work as group builders and may enclose empty expressions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150994				0	tg	A group operation does not have any effect on higher-level operations but rather affects only lower-level groups and expressions, including their brackets and super-/subscripts. For example, "a + size *2 (b * size -8 c_1)^2"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151392				0	tg	Set brackets were previously inserted in the Selection window or directly in the Commands window as "left lbrace <?> right rbrace". Now, a left and a right set bracket can also be inserted using "lbrace" and "rbrace", without or without wildcards.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152766				0	tg	This differs in different contexts: "x^{size 18 a_n}" and "x^{size *1.5 a_n}"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152952				0	tg	Group operations in sequence are treated as if every single operation is enclosed by braces. They are nested, and in every level there can be no more than one operation. Here is an example of a formula with many group operations: "size 12 color red font sans size -5 (a + size 8 b)" like "{size 12{color red{font sans{size -5 (a + {size 8 b})}}}}".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153002				0	tg	Since "left" and "right" ensure unique assignment of the brackets, every single bracket can be used as an argument for these two commands, even placing right brackets on the left side, or left brackets on the right. Instead of a bracket you can use the "none" qualifier, which means that there is no bracket shown and that there is no space reserved for a bracket. Using this, you can create the following expressions:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153153				0	tg	\\langle, \\rangle				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153354				0	tg	Note that all n here have different sizes. The size 1.333 results from 8/6, the desired size divided by the default index size 6. (Index size 50% with a base size of 12)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153532				0	tg	Please note that the quotation marks must be entered and can be obtained with \<emph\>Shift+2\</emph\> and not with typographical quotation marks. Generally, punctuation marks (like the comma in this case) are set as text. Although it is also possible to type "\\[2,~3\\)" the above option is preferable. In the previous example, "fixed size" always describes a bracket size dependent on the font size used.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153674				0	tg	Nesting groups within each other is relatively problem-free. In the formula hat "{a + b}" the "hat" is displayed simply over the center of "{a + b}". Also, "color red lceil a rceil" and "grave hat langle x * y rangle" work as expected. The result of the latter can be compared to "grave {hat langle x * y rangle}." These attributes do not compete, but rather can be combined.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153720				0	tg	The complete overview is as follows				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154136				0	tg	for "size +n", "size -n", "size *n", and "size /n" the effects of the operations are combined,				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154196				0	tg	"color ..." and "font ..." as well as "size n" (n is a decimal) replace any preceding operations of the same type				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154386				0	tg	x^{a_{size *1.333 n}}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154562				0	tg	Brackets do not adjust their size to the enclosed expression. For example, if you want "( a over b )" with a bracket size adjusted to a and b you must insert "left" and "right". Entering "left(a over b right)" produces appropriate sizing. If, however, the brackets themselves are part of the expression whose size is changed, they are included the size change: "size 3(a over b)" and "size 12(a over b)". The sizing of the bracket-to-bracket expression ratio does not change in any way.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154690				0	tg	\\lline, \\rline				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154906				0	tg	To change the size of a formula, use "size +" or -,*,/. Do not use "size n". These can easily be used in any context. This enables you to copy to other areas by using Copy and Paste, and the result remains the same. Furthermore, such expressions survive a change of base size in the menu better than when using "size n". If you use only "size *" and "size /" (for example, "size *1.24 a or size /0.86 a") the proportions remain intact.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155143				0	tg	a_{size *1.333 n}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155174				0	tg	"size *2 ( a + size /2 b )"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155761				0	tg	All types of brackets have the same grouping function as described for "{}"\<emph\>-\</emph\> brackets.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155989				0	tg	[2, 3) - right open interval				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156060				0	tg	left rangle x right lfloor				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157870				0	tg	All brackets may only be used in pairs. The brackets have some common characteristics:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157986				0	tg	Examples with size +n for a comparison. They look identical:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158001				0	tg	a_{size 8 n}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158441				0	tg	This example formula is then interpreted from left to right. The operations only affect its corresponding group (or expression). Operations further to the right "replace" or "combine themselves with" their predecessors.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tg	Brackets and Grouping				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150746				0	tg	\<bookmark_value\>indexes and exponents in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponents and indexes in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150746				0	tg	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091200.xhp\" name=\"Indexes and Exponents\"\>Indexes and Exponents\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145207				0	tg	a_{2^3}^{4_5}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147516				0	tg	Super- and subscripts to the left of the base character can also be right-justified. To do this, the new commands "lsub" and "lsup" are used. Both commands have the same effect as "sub" and "sup", except that they are left of the base character. See also "a lsub 2 lsup 3."				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148387				0	tg	However, it is no longer possible to use the following patterns				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149029				0	tg	a_2_3				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149884				0	tg	The index and exponent for a character are displayed one on top of the other, left-justified to the base character. For example, type \<emph\>a_2^3\</emph\> or \<emph\>a^3_2\</emph\>. This can be in any order. Instead of \<emph\>'_'\</emph\> and \<emph\>'^'\</emph\>, you can use \<emph\>'sub'\</emph\> and \<emph\>'sup'\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151173	13			0	tg	Unlike other formula editors where "\<emph\>_\</emph\>" and " \<emph\>^\</emph\> " only refer to the next character ("a_24" refers only to the "2"), $[officename] Math refers to the entire number(s)/name(s)/text. If you want to put superscripts and subscripts in sequence, the expression can be written as follows: a_2{}^3 or a^3{}_2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151185				0	tg	a_{2_3}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151272				0	tg	a^{2^3}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152961				0	tg	The commands "sub" and "sup" are also available as "rsub" and "rsup".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153724				0	tg	Each sub-/superscript position of a base character can only be used once. You must use brackets to indicate the desired result. The following examples illustrate this				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153730				0	tg	Here, you will find basic information about indexes and exponents in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\>. You can try the examples described here to help you understand the details discussed. (The quotation marks in this text are for emphasis purposes only and are not part of the examples.)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153923				0	tg	a_2^3_4				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154260				0	tg	To write tensors, \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> provides several options. In addition to the notation "R_i{}^{jk}{}_l", common in other applications, additional notations can be used, namely "R_i{}^jk{}_l" and "{{R_i}^jk}_l."				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154276				0	tg	The rules governing unambiguity and the necessity of using brackets remain the same. In principle, this can be achieved with \<emph\>{}_2^3 a\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154570				0	tg	Super- and subscripts can be attached to most unary and binary operators. Two examples: "a div_2 b a<csub n b +_2 h" and "a toward csub f b x toward csup f y."				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155904				0	tg	Be sure to also enter all spaces between characters when entering these examples into the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155985				0	tg	a^2^3				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156316				0	tg	a_2^{3_4}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3158437				0	tg	Using the "csub" and "csup" commands, you can write super- and subscripts directly above or below a character. An example is "a csub y csup x". Combinations of indexes and exponents together are also possible: "abc_1^2 lsub 3 lsup 4 csub 55555 csup 66666."				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tg	Indexes and Exponents				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148839				0	tg	\<bookmark_value\>attributes; additional information\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148839				0	tg	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091300.xhp\" name=\"Attributes\"\>Attributes\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146966				0	tg	The \<emph\>acute\</emph\>, \<emph\>bar\</emph\>, \<emph\>breve\</emph\>, \<emph\>check\</emph\>, \<emph\>circle\</emph\>, \<emph\>dot\</emph\>, \<emph\>ddot\</emph\>, \<emph\>dddot\</emph\>, \<emph\>grave\</emph\>, \<emph\>hat\</emph\>, \<emph\>tilde\</emph\> and \<emph\>vec\</emph\> attributes always have a fixed size and do not become wider (longer) if they are above a long symbol. By default, the attributes are centered.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150051				0	tg	Additional information about attributes in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> is found here.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151180				0	tg	For some character strings, it is possible that a line inserted with \<emph\>underline\</emph\> is too close to the character. In this case, an empty group can solve the problem: \<emph\>underline Q sub {}\</emph\> instead of \<emph\>underline Q\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154557				0	tg	The only attributes which grow with the length of the symbol are \<emph\>overline\</emph\>, \<emph\>underline\</emph\> and \<emph\>overstrike\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tg	Атрибутҳо				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153923				0	tg	\<bookmark_value\>scaling; in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153923				0	tg	 \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091400.xhp\" name=\"Scaling\"\>Scaling\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3145206				0	tg	Some \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091300.xhp\" name=\"Attributes\"\>Attributes\</link\> have fixed sizes; do not change these if they are placed above a long symbol.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3147173				0	tg	More detailed information about scaling in \<emph\>\<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Math\</emph\> as well as some examples can be found here. (The quotation marks in this text are for emphasis purposes only and are not part of the examples.)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3147570				0	tg	Brackets preceded by "left" or "right", however, are always adjusted to the argument. See "left(left(left(a right)right)right)", "left(stack{a#b#c}right)", "left(a over b right)."				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3150756				0	tg	Brackets always have a fixed size as well. This applies to all symbols that can be used as brackets. Compare "(((a)))", "( stack{a#b#c})", "(a over b)."				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3154694				0	tg	The spaces in the examples are required for the correct structure. You may not delete them when making entries in the Commands window.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3156316				0	tg	The factorial is not scaled (example: "fact stack{a#b}" and "fact {a over b}") but is oriented using the baseline or center of the arguments.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tg	Масштабноккунӣ				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091500.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155961				0	tg	\<bookmark_value\>$[officename] Math;reference list\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas;reference tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>reference tables; formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>operators;in Math\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155961				0	tg	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091500.xhp\" name=\"Formula Reference Tables\"\>Formula Reference Tables\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091500.xhp	0	help	par_id3149502				0	tg	\<variable id=\"ref\"\>This reference section contains lists of many operators, functions, symbols and formatting features available in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\>. Many of the commands displayed can be inserted using the icons in the \<emph\>Selection\</emph\> window or the context menu of the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window. \</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
