helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150099				0	tg	\<bookmark_value\>searching; with wildcards\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>regular expressions;searching\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>wildcards; searching in text\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>examples for regular expressions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150099				0	tg	\<variable id=\"search_regexp\"\>\<link href=\"text/swriter/guide/search_regexp.xhp\"\>Using Wildcards in Text Searches\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153401				0	tg	Regular Expression Examples				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	par_id2142399				0	tg	Click \<emph\>More Options\</emph\> to expand the dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	par_id3149609				0	tg	The wildcard combination to search for zero or more occurrences of any character is a period and asterisk (.*).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	par_id3149641				0	tg	The wildcard for a single character is a period (.).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	par_id3149843				0	tg	In the \<emph\>Search for \</emph\>box, type the search term and the wildcard(s) that you want to use in your search.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	par_id3149854				0	tg	The wildcard for the end of a paragraph is a dollar sign ($). The wildcard character combination for the start of a paragraph is a caret and a period (^.).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	par_id3149875				0	tg	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/02100001.xhp\" name=\"List of Wildcards\"\>List of Wildcards\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	par_id3153136				0	tg	The wildcard for zero or more occurrences of the previous character is an asterisk. For example: "123*" finds "12" "123", and "1233".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	par_id3153414				0	tg	You can only search for regular expressions within the same paragraph. That is, you cannot search for one term in a paragraph and a different term in the next paragraph.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	par_id3155182				0	tg	You can use wildcards when you find and replace text in a document. For example, "s.n" finds "sun" and "son".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	par_id3155861				0	tg	Select the \<emph\>Regular expressions\</emph\> check box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	par_id3155907				0	tg	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Find & Replace\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	par_id3156113				0	tg	Click \<emph\>Find \</emph\>or\<emph\> Find All\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\search_regexp.xhp	0	help	tit				0	tg	Using Wildcards in Text Searches				2002-02-02 02:02:02
